### PRIORITY P9: Antimicrobial Stewardship and Infection Prevention and Control

**Rationale for inclusion:** Winter is peak season for respiratory and gastrointestinal infections, Norovirus outbreaks close hospital wards, and inappropriate antibiotic prescribing for viral upper respiratory tract infections drives AMR. The UK 5-Year AMR National Action Plan and NHS ARHAI programme address this. IPC measures (hand hygiene, PPE, cohorting) remain critical post-COVID.

| Criterion | Score (1-5) | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Magnitude of winter NHS problem | 4 | Norovirus, influenza, and MRSA/C.diff outbreaks are major winter disruptors; ward closures directly reduce capacity |
| Strength of evidence | 4 | Strong evidence for AMS programmes reducing inappropriate prescribing (Davey et al., 2017 Cochrane); IPC evidence robust for hand hygiene and contact precautions |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | AMS programmes demonstrably cost-saving; IPC investment prevents expensive HAI outbreaks |
| NHS/Life Sciences alignment | 4 | UK AMR National Action Plan; NICE guidelines on AMS (NG15); NHS ARHAI standards |
| Temporal impact | 3 | Immediate IPC impact; AMS impact on AMR accrues over years |
| Health inequalities impact | 2 | Less directly linked to inequality agenda; HAIs somewhat linked to socioeconomic factors through underlying health status |

