### PRIORITY P12: Medicines Optimisation and Structured Medication Review (SMR)

**Rationale for inclusion:** Approximately 6–7% of hospital admissions in England are drug-related, with a significant proportion preventable (Howard et al., 2007). Polypharmacy is endemic in older adults with multimorbidity and contributes to falls, adverse drug reactions, and acute admissions. Structured Medication Review (SMR), delivered by clinical pharmacists in PCNs, provides a systematic approach to rationalising medicines and improving adherence.

| Criterion | Score (1-5) | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Magnitude of winter NHS problem | 4 | Drug-related admissions peak in winter alongside other illness; COPD/heart failure medication adherence directly impacts winter stability |
| Strength of evidence | 3 | SMR evidence mixed; clinical pharmacist prescribing evidence positive for specific conditions; polypharmacy review RCT evidence inconclusive (Patterson et al., 2014) |
| Cost-effectiveness | 3 | Drug-related admissions cost significant NHS resource; SMR cost-effectiveness modelling positive but real-world NHS data limited |
| NHS/Life Sciences alignment | 4 | PCN clinical pharmacist DES; NICE medicines optimisation guidance (NG5); NHS STOPP/START tool implementation |
| Temporal impact | 3 | Medium-term benefit; requires pharmacist workforce development |
| Health inequalities impact | 3 | Polypharmacy disproportionate in deprived older populations; SMR can directly address this if accessible |

